contentText:
Don&#8217;t call Molly Santana &#8220;The Queen of the Underground.&#8221;

The West Coast rapper recently sat down with Bootleg Kev to promote her upcoming album Black Punk and pushed back when Kev referred to her as &#8220;The Queen of the Underground.&#8221;

&#8220;I don&#8217;t want to be queen of the underground,&#8221; she said before expressing that she feels like that particular scene isn&#8217;t exactly welcoming to women. &#8220;They&#8217;re mean&#8230; Bro, it&#8217;s rough. They spot that I be in, it&#8217;s rough. Not to be like so negative, but the underground doesn&#8217;t really like women. But we already knew that.&#8221;

&#8220;It&#8217;s such a love/hate relationship,&#8221; Santana continued.

Elsewhere in the interview, she talked about being featured on Iceman&#8217;s &#8220;Ran to Atlanta&#8221; with Drake and Future and was asked who she was &#8220;more stoked&#8221; to be on the song with. &#8220;Equally geeked up, bro,&#8221; she said. &#8220;The thing is, bro, both speak to my soul. What do you mean? Those are my f—kin&#8217; GOATS, bruh. You pull up songs from them and I will sing them with my whole spirit and head and toes, screaming.

Adding, &#8220;I just appreciate the performance art. Both of them have devoted their life to what they do, and that&#8217;s real. That&#8217;s some real popstar s—t.&#8221;

When talking with The Cruz Show  at Real 92.3 L.A. , she admitted to liking her verse on &#8220;Ran to Atlanta&#8221; at first, saying that she recorded her verse &#8220;maybe like five times, six times&#8221; and didn&#8217;t want Drake and his team to pick the version that made the album.

“I was just doing too much,” she said about some of her initial takes. “And I listened back to the other verses and I&#8217;m like, &#8216;What was I doing? Why was I trying to do too much? It&#8217;s okay. That’s not me. That&#8217;s just not you. You gotta be you.&#8217; And so, the verse that they picked was the most me.&#8221;

However, she did confess to being a little annoyed by the process.

&#8220;I was like, ‘I don’t know why they like this,&#8217; she continued. &#8220;He was really fighting me to do that version, and when I was doing it I literally had the worst attitude. I was so pissed off, I was like, ‘This is not me…’ But sometimes you don’t know yourself ‘cause you’re growing up, and you get put in a spot of crazy pressure… I’m glad that I had them around to put me back in my element and set me up for, like, to find a whole new flow for myself.”

Black Punk is set to release later this month on Black Friday, Nov. 27.

